= In Praise of Idleness / 懒惰颂

#html.p(id: "en-S001")[#text("Like most of my generation, I was brought up on the saying: \"Satan finds some mischief still for idle hands to do.\" Being a highly virtuous child, I believed all that I was told, and acquired a conscience which has kept me working hard down to the present moment. But although my conscience has controlled my actions, my opinions have undergone a revolution. I think that there is far too much work done in the world, that immense harm is caused by the belief that work is virtuous, and that what needs to be preached in modern industrial countries is quite different from what always has been preached. Everyone knows the story of the traveller in Naples who saw twelve beggars lying in the sun (it was before the days of Mussolini), and offered a lira to the laziest of them. Eleven of them jumped up to claim it, so he gave it to the twelfth. This traveller was on the right lines. But in countries which do not enjoy Mediterranean sunshine idleness is more difficult, and a great public propaganda will be required to inaugurate it. I hope that, after reading the following pages, the leaders of the Y.M.C.A. will start a campaign to induce good young men to do nothing. If so, I shall not have lived in vain.")]

#zh(id: "S001")[#text("同我这一代的大多数人一样，我从小听着这句话长大：‘魔鬼总会给闲着的双手找点坏事干。’我小时候品德极好，别人告诉我的一切都相信，于是养成了一种良心，让我直到今天仍不停地努力工作。然而，尽管这良心支配着我的行动，我的观点却发生了一场革命。我认为，世界上做的工作太多了，把工作视为美德的信念造成了巨大的伤害；现代工业国家需要宣扬的东西，与过去一直宣扬的很不相同。大家都知道那个去那不勒斯旅行的人的故事：他看见十二个乞丐躺在太阳底下——那还是墨索里尼时代以前——便说，要给最懒的一个人一里拉。十一人立刻跳起来争要，他便把钱给了第十二人。这位旅行者的思路是对的。但在没有地中海阳光可享的国家，懒惰比较难实行，要提倡它，就得展开大规模的宣传。我希望基督教青年会的领导人读完以下几页，便发起一场运动，劝好青年们什么也别干。若能如此，我就不算白活了。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S002")[#text("Before advancing my own arguments for laziness, I must dispose of one which I cannot accept. Whenever a person who already has enough to live on proposes to engage in some everyday kind of job, such as school-teaching or typing, he or she is told that such conduct takes the bread out of other people's mouths, and is therefore wicked. If this argument were valid, it would only be necessary for us all to be idle in order that we should all have our mouths full of bread. What people who say such things forget is that what a man earns he usually spends, and in spending he gives employment. As long as a man spends his income, he puts just as much bread into people's mouths in spending as he takes out of other people's mouths in earning. The real villain, from this point of view, is the man who saves. If he merely puts his savings in a stocking, like the proverbial French peasant, it is obvious that they do not give employment. If he invests his savings, the matter is less obvious, and different cases arise.")]

#zh(id: "S002")[#text("在提出我自己的懒惰论点之前，我必须先处理掉一个我无法接受的论点。每当一个已经拥有足够生活所需的人打算从事一些日常工作，例如学校教学或打字时，他或她就会被告知这种行为会从别人嘴里夺走面包，因此是邪恶的。如果这个论点是正确的，那么我们只需要无所事事，就能让我们的嘴里充满面包。说这些话的人忘记了，一个人赚到的钱通常会花掉，而在消费的同时他也提供了就业机会。只要一个人花掉自己的收入，他在花钱时送进别人嘴里的面包就和他在挣钱时从别人嘴里拿走的面包一样多。从这个角度来看，真正的恶棍是拯救者。如果他只是像众所周知的法国农民一样将积蓄放在袜子里，那么显然他们并没有提供就业机会。如果他用自己的积蓄进行投资，问题就不那么明显了，并且会出现不同的情况。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S003")[#text("One of the commonest things to do with savings is to lend them to some Government. In view of the fact that the bulk of the public expenditure of most civilized Governments consists in payment for past wars or preparation for future wars, the man who lends his money to a Government is in the same position as the bad men in Shakespeare who hire murderers. The net result of the man's economical habits is to increase the armed forces of the State to which he lends his savings. Obviously it would be better if he spent the money, even if he spent it in drink or gambling.")]

#zh(id: "S003")[#text("储蓄最常见的事情之一是将其借给某个政府。鉴于大多数文明政府的大部分公共支出都用于支付过去的战争或为未来的战争做准备，借钱给政府的人与莎士比亚笔下雇佣杀人犯的坏人处于同样的地位。该人的经济习惯的最终结果是增加他借出储蓄的国家的武装部队。显然这些钱还是花掉比较好，哪怕是用来喝酒或者赌博。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S004")[#text("But, I shall be told, the case is quite different when savings are invested in industrial enterprises. When such enterprises succeed, and produce something useful, this may be conceded. In these days, however, no one will deny that most enterprises fail. That means that a large amount of human labour, which might have been devoted to producing something that could be enjoyed, was expended on producing machines which, when produced, lay idle and did no good to anyone. The man who invests his savings in a concern that goes bankrupt is therefore injuring others as well as himself. If he spent his money, say, in giving parties for his friends, they (we may hope) would get pleasure, and so would all those upon whom he spent money, such as the butcher, the baker, and the bootlegger. But if he spends it (let us say) upon laying down rails for surface cars in some place where surface cars turn out to be not wanted, he has diverted a mass of labour into channels where it gives pleasure to no one. Nevertheless, when he becomes poor through the failure of his investment he will be regarded as a victim of undeserved misfortune, whereas the gay spendthrift, who has spent his money philanthropically, will be despised as a fool and a frivolous person.")]

#zh(id: "S004")[#text("但是，我应该知道，当储蓄投资于工业企业时，情况就完全不同了。当这些企业成功并生产出有用的东西时，这一点可能会被承认。然而，如今，没有人会否认大多数企业都失败了。这意味着大量的人类劳动原本可以用来生产可以享受的东西，但却被用来生产机器，而这些机器一旦生产出来，就闲置起来，对任何人都没有好处。因此，将自己的积蓄投资于一家即将破产的公司的人不仅伤害了自己，也伤害了他人。例如，如果他花钱为他的朋友举办聚会，他们（我们可能希望）会感到高兴，所有他花钱的人也会如此，例如屠夫、面包师和走私者。但是，如果他把钱（比方说）花在一些不需要地面车辆的地方铺设地面车辆的铁轨上，那么他就将大量劳动力转移到了任何人都不会感到高兴的渠道中。然而，当他因投资失败而变得贫穷时，他会被视为不应有的不幸的受害者，而那些把钱花在慈善事业上的同性恋挥霍者会被视为傻瓜和轻浮的人。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S005")[#text("All this is only preliminary. I want to say, in all seriousness, that a great deal of harm is being done in the modern world by belief in the virtuousness Of WORK, and that the road to happiness and prosperity lies in an organized diminution of work.")]

#zh(id: "S005")[#text("这一切都只是初步的。我想说，严肃地说，在现代世界中，对工作美德的信仰正在造成很大的伤害，而通往幸福和繁荣的道路在于有组织地减少工作。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S006")[#text("First of all: what is work? Work is of two kinds: first, altering the position of matter at or near the earth's surface relatively to other such matter; second, telling other people to do so. The first kind is unpleasant and ill paid; the second is pleasant and highly paid. The second kind is capable of indefinite extension: there are not only those who give orders, but those who give advice as to what orders should be given. Usually two opposite kinds of advice are given simultaneously by two organized bodies of men; this is called politics. The skill required for this,kind of work is not knowledge of the subjects as to which advice is given, but know- ledge of the art of persuasive speaking and writing, i.e. of advertising.")]

#zh(id: "S006")[#text("首先：什么是工作？功有两种：第一，改变地球表面或附近物质相对于其他物质的位置；第二，改变地球表面或附近物质相对于其他物质的位置；第二，告诉其他人这样做。第一种是令人不愉快且报酬低的；第二个是令人愉快且报酬高。第二种是可以无限期延伸的：不仅有发号施令的人，还有就应该下达什么命令提出建议的人。通常，两个有组织的团体会同时提出两种相反的建议。这就是所谓的政治。此类工作所需的技能不是提供建议的主题知识，而是说服性演讲和写作艺术（即广告艺术）的知识。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S007")[#text("Throughout Europe, though not in America, there is a third class of men, more respected than either of the classes of workers. There are men who, through ownership of land, are able to make others pay for the privilege of being allowed to exist and to work. These landowners are idle., and I might therefore be expected to praise them. Unfortunately, their idleness is only rendered possible by the industry of others; indeed their desire for comfortable idleness is historically the source of the whole gospel of work. The last thing they have ever wished is that others should follow their example.")]

#zh(id: "S007")[#text("在整个欧洲，虽然在美国没有，但存在着第三类人，他们比任何一个工人阶级都更受尊重。有些人通过拥有土地，能够让其他人为被允许生存和工作的特权付出代价。这些地主很闲，因此我可能会赞扬他们。不幸的是，他们的闲散只有通过其他人的勤奋才可能实现。事实上，从历史上看，他们对舒适闲散的渴望是整个工作福音的源泉。他们最不希望的就是其他人效仿他们的榜样。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S008")[#text("From the beginning of civilization until the Industrial Revolution, a man could, as a rule, produce by hard work little more than was required for the subsistence of himself and his family, although his wife worked at least as hard as he did,, and his children added their labour as soon as they were old enough to do so. The small surplus above bare necessaries was not left to those who produced it, but was appropriated by warriors and priests. In times of famine there was no surplus; the warriors and priests, however, still secured as much as at other times, with the result that many of the workers died of hunger. This system persisted in Russia until I9I7 (1), I and still persists in the East; in England, in spite of the Industrial Revolution, it remained in full force throughout the Napoleonic wars, and until a hundred years ago, when the new class of manufacturers acquired power. In America, the system came to an end with the Revolution, except in the South, where it persisted until the Civil War. A system which lasted so long and ended so recently has naturally left a profound impress upon men's thoughts and opinions. Much that we take for granted about the desirability of work is derived from this system, and, being pre-industrial, is not adapted to the modern world. Modern technique has made it possible for leisure, within limits, to be not the prerogative of small privileged classes, tut a right evenly distributed throughout the community. The morality of work is the morality of slaves, and the modern world has no need of slavery.")]

#zh(id: "S008")[#text("从文明开始到工业革命，一般来说，一个人通过辛勤工作所获得的产出仅够维持他自己和家人的生存所需，尽管他的妻子至少和他一样努力工作，而且他的孩子们一到了可以工作的年龄就加入了他们的劳动。基本生活必需品之上的少量盈余并没有留给生产者，而是由战士和牧师侵占。饥荒时，没有余粮；然而，战士和牧师仍然像其他时候一样受到保护，结果许多工人死于饥饿。这种制度在俄罗斯一直持续到I9I7(1)，并且在东方仍然存在；在英国，尽管发生了工业革命，但它在整个拿破仑战争期间仍然充分发挥作用，直到一百年前新的制造商阶级获得权力为止。在美国，这一制度随着革命而结束，但在南方除外，它一直持续到内战。一个持续了这么久、最近才结束的制度自然给人们的思想和观点留下了深刻的印象。我们认为工作的可取性是理所当然的，很多都源于这个体系，而且由于是前工业时代，并不适应现代世界。现代技术使得休闲在一定限度内不再是少数特权阶级的特权，而是一种均匀分布在整个社会的权利。工作的道德是奴隶的道德，现代世界不需要奴隶制。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S009")[#text("It is obvious that, in primitive communities, peasants, left to themselves, would not have parted with the slender surplus upon which the warriors and priests subsisted, but would have either produced less or consumed more. At first, sheer force compelled them to produce and part with the surplus. Gradually, however, it was found possible to induce many of them to accept an ethic according to which it was their duty to work hard, although part of their work went to support others in idleness. By this means the amount of compulsion required was lessened, and the expenses of government were diminished. To this day, 99 per cent of British wage-earners would be genuinely shocked if it were proposed that the King should not have a larger income than a working man. The conception of duty, speaking historically, has been a means used by the holders of power to induce others to live for the interests of their masters rather than for their own. Of course the holders of power conceal this fact from themselves by managing to believe that their interests are identical with the larger interests of humanity. Sometimes this is true; Athenian slave-owners, for instance, employed part of their leisure in making a permanent contribution to civilization which would have been impossible under a just economic system. Leisure is essential to civilization, and in former times leisure for the few was only rendered possible by the labours of the many. But their labours were valuable., not because work is good, but because leisure is good. And with modern technique it would be possible to distribute leisure justly without injury to civilization.")]

#zh(id: "S009")[#text("显然，在原始社会中，如果农民自生自灭，他们不会放弃战士和祭司赖以生存的微薄剩余，但要么生产更少，要么消费更多。起初，纯粹的武力迫使他们生产并分配剩余的产品。然而，渐渐地，人们发现，有可能引导他们中的许多人接受一种道德，根据这种道德，努力工作是他们的责任，尽管他们的部分工作用于养活闲散的其他人。通过这种方式，所需的强制程度减少了，政府的开支也减少了。直到今天，如果有人提出国王的收入不应高于工人的收入，99%的英国工薪阶层都会感到震惊。从历史上看，义务的概念一直是权力持有者用来诱导他人为主人的利益而不是为自己的利益而生活的手段。当然，掌权者通过设法相信自己的利益与人类的更大利益是一致的来向自己隐瞒这一事实。有时这是真的；有时这是真的。例如，雅典奴隶主利用部分闲暇为文明做出永久贡献，这在公正的经济制度下是不可能的。休闲对于文明至关重要，而在过去，少数人的休闲只有通过多数人的劳动才能实现。但他们的劳动是有价值的，并不是因为工作固然好，但因为休闲也好。借助现代技术，可以在不损害文明的情况下公正地分配闲暇时间。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S010")[#text("Modern technique has made it possible to diminish enormously the amount of labour required to secure the necessaries of life for everyone. This was made obvious during the war. At that time, all the men in the armed forces, all the men and women engaged in the production of munitions, all the men and women engaged in spying, war propaganda, or Government offices connected with the war, were withdrawn from productive occupations. In spite of this, the general level of physical well-being among unskilled wage-earners on the side of the Allies was higher than before or since. The significance of this fact was concealed by finance: borrowing made it appear as if the future was nourishing the present. But that, of course, would have been impossible; a man cannot eat a loaf of bread that does not yet exist. The war showed conclusively that, by the scientific organization of production, it is possible to keep modern populations in fair comfort on a small part of the working capacity of the modern world. If, at the end of the war, the scientific organization, which had been created in order to liberate men for fighting and munition work, had been preserved, and the hours of work had been cut down to four, all would have been well. Instead of that the old chaos was restored, those whose work was demanded were made to work long hours, and the rest were left to starve as unemployed. Why? because work is a duty, and a man should not receive wages in proportion to what he has produced, but in proportion to his virtue as exemplified by his industry.")]

#zh(id: "S010")[#text("现代技术可以极大地减少保障每个人生活必需品所需的劳动力。这一点在战争期间表现得很明显。那时，武装部队中的所有男人、所有从事军火生产的男人和女人、所有从事间谍活动、战争宣传或与战争有关的政府办公室的男人和女人，都从生产性职业中撤出。尽管如此，盟军一方的非熟练工薪阶层的总体身体健康水平比以前或之后要高。这一事实的重要性被金融掩盖了：借贷让未来看起来像是在滋养现在。但这当然是不可能的。一个人不能吃一条还不存在的面包。战争最终表明，通过科学的生产组织，可以用现代世界的一小部分劳动能力使现代人口过上舒适的生活。如果在战争结束时，为了解放人们从事战斗和弹药工作而建立的科学组织得以保留，并且工作时间减少到四小时，一切都会好起来的。那些需要工作的人并没有恢复原来的混乱状态，而是被迫长时间工作，而其余的人则因失业而挨饿。为什么？因为工作是一种责任，一个人不应该按照他所生产的东西来领取工资，但与他的勤奋所体现的美德成正比。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S011")[#text("This is the morality of the Slave State, applied in circumstances totally unlike those in which it arose. No wonder the result has been disastrous. Let us take an illustration. Suppose that, at a given moment, a certain number of people are engaged in the manufacture of pins. They make as many pins as the world needs, working (say) eight hours a day. Someone makes an invention by which the same number of men can make twice as many pins as before. But the world does not need twice as many pins: pins arc already so cheap that hardly any more will be bought at a lower price. In a sensible world., everybody concerned in the manufacture of pins would take to working four hours instead of eight, and everything else would go on as before. But in the actual world this would be thought demoralizing. The men still work eight hours, there are too many pins, some employers go bankrupt, and half the men previously concerned in making pins are thrown out of work. There is, in the end, just as much leisure as on the other plan, but half the men are totally idle while half are still overworked. In this way, it is insured that the unavoidable leisure shall cause misery all round instead of being a universal source of happiness. Can anything more insane be imagined?")]

#zh(id: "S011")[#text("这就是奴隶国的道德，适用于与它产生时完全不同的环境。难怪结果是灾难性的。让我们举个例子。假设在某个特定时刻，有一定数量的人从事别针的制造。他们生产世界需要的数量的别针，每天工作（比如说）八小时。有人发明了一项发明，用同样数量的人可以制作比以前多一倍的别针。但世界并不需要两倍的别针：别针已经很便宜了，几乎不会再以更低的价格购买了。在一个明智的世界里，每个参与别针制造的人都会花四个小时而不是八个小时工作，而其他一切都会像以前一样进行。但在现实世界中，这会被认为是士气低落。这些人仍然工作八小时，别针太多，一些雇主破产，以前从事制作别针的人有一半失业了。最终，与另一个计划中的闲暇时间一样多，但一半的人完全无所事事，而一半的人仍然过度劳累。这样一来，不可避免的闲暇就注定会带来痛苦，而不是普遍的幸福源泉。还能想象更疯狂的事情吗？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S012")[#text("The idea that the poor should have leisure has always been shocking to the rich. In England, in the early nineteenth century, fifteen hours was the ordinary day's work for a man; children sometimes did as much, and very commonly did twelve hours a day. When meddlesome busybodies suggested that perhaps these hours were rather long, they were told that work kept adults from drink and children from mischief. When I was a child, shortly after urban working men had acquired the vote, certain public holidays were established by law, to the great indignation of the upper classes. I remember hearing an old Duchess say: \"What do the poor want with holidays? They ought to work.\" People nowadays are less frank, but the sentiment persists, and is the source of much of our economic confusion.")]

#zh(id: "S012")[#text("穷人应该有闲暇的想法一直让富人感到震惊。在十九世纪初的英国，十五个小时是一个人每天的正常工作时间。孩子们有时也会做同样的事情，而且很常见的是一天做十二个小时。当爱管闲事的人提出也许这些时间相当长时，他们被告知工作使大人不能喝酒，孩子也不能恶作剧。当我还是个孩子的时候，城市工人获得选举权后不久，法律就规定了某些公共假期，引起了上层阶级的极大愤慨。我记得听一位老公爵夫人说过：“穷人想要假期做什么？他们应该工作。”现在的人们不那么坦率了，但这种情绪依然存在，并且是我们经济混乱的根源。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S013")[#text("Let us, for a moment, consider the ethics of work frankly, without superstition. Every human being, of necessity, consumes, in the course of his life, a certain amount of the produce of human labour. Assuming, as we may, that labour is on the whole disagreeable, it is unjust that a man should consume more than he produces. Of course he may provide services rather than commodities, like a medical man, for example; but he should provide something in return for his board and lodging. To this extent, the duty of work must be admitted, but to this extent only.")]

#zh(id: "S013")[#text("让我们暂时坦诚地思考一下工作道德，不要迷信。每个人在其一生中都不可避免地消耗一定数量的人类劳动产品。我们可以假设，劳动总体上是令人不快的，那么一个人的消费多于他的生产就是不公平的。当然，他可以提供服务而不是商品，例如像医生；但他应该提供一些东西作为他的食宿的回报。在这个程度上，必须承认工作义务，但仅在这个程度上。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S014")[#text("I shall not dwell upon the fact that, in all modern societies outside the U.S.S.R., many people escape even this minimum of work, namely all those who inherit money and all those who marry money. I do not think the fact that these people are allowed to be idle is nearly so harmful as the fact that wage-earners are expected to overwork or starve.")]

#zh(id: "S014")[#text("我不会详述这样一个事实：在苏联以外的所有现代社会中，许多人甚至逃避了这一最低限度的工作，即所有继承金钱的人和所有与金钱结婚的人。我认为，允许这些人无所事事的事实远不如工薪阶层过度工作或挨饿的事实那么有害。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S015")[#text("If the ordinary wage-earner worked four hours a day, there would be enough for everybody, and no unemployment-assuming a certain very moderate amount of sensible organization. This idea shocks the well-to-do., because they are convinced that the poor would not know how to use so much leisure. In America, men often work long hours even when they are already well off; such men, naturally, are indignant at the idea of leisure for wage-earners, except as the grim punishment of unemployment; in fact, they dislike leisure even for their sons. Oddly enough, while they wish their sons to work so hard as to have no time to be civilized, they do not mind their wives and daughters having no work at all. The snobbish admiration of uselessness, which, in an aristocratic society, extends to both sexes, is, under a plutocracy, confined to women; this, however, does not make it any more in agreement with common sense.")]

#zh(id: "S015")[#text("如果普通工薪阶层每天工作四个小时，那么每个人就足够了，而且不会失业——假设有一定数量的合理组织。这个想法让富人感到震惊，因为他们相信穷人不知道如何利用这么多的闲暇。在美国，男性即使已经很富裕，也经常工作很长时间。这些人自然会对工薪阶层的闲暇感到愤慨，除非这是对失业的严厉惩罚。事实上，他们甚至不喜欢自己的儿子有闲暇。奇怪的是，虽然他们希望自己的儿子努力工作，没有时间讲文明，但他们并不介意自己的妻子和女儿没有工作。对无用的势利崇拜，在贵族社会中延伸到男女双方，而在财阀统治下，则仅限于女性。然而，这并不意味着它就不再符合常识。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S016")[#text("The wise use of leisure, it must be conceded, is a product of civilization and education. A man who has worked long hours all his life will be bored if he becomes suddenly idle. But without a consider- able amount of leisure a man is cut off from many of the best things. There is no longer any reason why the bulk of the population should suffer this deprivation; only a foolish asceticism, usually vicarious., makes us continue to insist on work in excessive quantities now that the need no longer exists.")]

#zh(id: "S016")[#text("必须承认，明智地利用休闲是文明和教育的产物。一个长时间工作了一辈子的人，如果突然变得无所事事，就会感到无聊。但是，如果没有足够的闲暇，一个人就无法获得许多最好的东西。大部分人口不再有任何理由遭受这种剥夺；只有一种愚蠢的禁欲主义，通常是替代性的，使我们在不再需要的情况下继续坚持过多的工作。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S017")[#text("In the new creed which controls the government of Russia, while there is much that is very different from the traditional teaching of the West, there are some things that are quite unchanged. The attitude of the governing classes, and especially of those who conduct educational propaganda, on the subject of the dignity of labour, is almost exactly that which the governing classes of the world have always preached to what were called the \"honest poor.\" Industry, sobriety, willingness to work long hours for distant advantages, even submissiveness to authority, all these reappear; moreover authority still represents the will of the Ruler of the Universe, Who, however, is now called by a new name, Dialectical Materialism.")]

#zh(id: "S017")[#text("在控制俄罗斯政府的新信条中，虽然有很多与西方传统教义截然不同的内容，但也有一些东西是完全没有改变的。统治阶级，特别是那些进行教育宣传的人，在劳动尊严问题上的态度，几乎正是世界上统治阶级一直向所谓“诚实的穷人”鼓吹的态度。勤奋、清醒、为了遥远的利益而愿意长时间工作，甚至对权威的服从，所有这些都再次出现；此外，权威仍然代表宇宙统治者的意志，然而，他现在被称为新名字：辩证唯物主义。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S018")[#text("The victory of the proletariat in Russia has some points in common with the victory of the feminists in some other countries. For ages, men had conceded the superior saintliness of women, and had consoled women for their inferiority by maintaining that saintliness is more desirable than power. At last the feminists decided that they would have both, since the pioneers among them believed all that the men had told them about the desirability of virtue, but not what they had told them about the worthlessness of political power. A similar thing has happened in Russia as regards manual work. For ages, the rich and their sycophants have written in praise of \"honest toil,\" have praised the simple life, have professed a religion which teaches that the poor are much more likely to go to heaven than the rich, and in general have tried to make manual workers believe that there is some special nobility about altering the position of matter in space, just as men tried to make women believe that they derived some special nobility from their sexual enslavement. In Russia, all this teaching about the excellence of manual work has been taken seriously, with the result that the manual worker is more honoured than anyone else. What are, in essence, revivalist appeals are made, but not for the old purposes: they are made to secure shock workers for special tasks. Manual work is the ideal which is held before the young, and is the basis of all ethical teaching.")]

#zh(id: "S018")[#text("俄罗斯无产阶级的胜利与其他一些国家女权主义者的胜利有一些共同点。长久以来，男人承认女性具有优越的圣洁性，并通过坚称圣洁比权力更令人向往来安慰她们的自卑。最后，女权主义者决定两者兼而有之，因为她们中的先驱者相信男人告诉她们的关于美德的可取性的一切，但不相信她们告诉她们的关于政治权力的无价值。在体力劳动方面，俄罗斯也发生了类似的事情。多年来，富人和他们的追随者一直在写作中赞扬“诚实的劳动”，赞扬简单的生活，信奉一种宗教，教导穷人比富人更容易上天堂，并且总体上试图让体力劳动者相信改变物质在空间中的位置存在某种特殊的高贵，就像男人试图让女人相信他们从性奴役中获得了某种特殊的高贵一样。在俄罗斯，所有关于体力劳动卓越性的教导都受到认真对待，结果体力劳动者比其他任何人都更受尊敬。从本质上讲，复兴主义的呼吁是出于旧的目的：它们是为了确保突击队员执行特殊任务。体力劳动是年轻人的理想，也是一切道德教育的基础。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S019")[#text("For the present, possibly, this is all to the good. A large country, full of natural resources, awaits development, and has to be developed with very little use of credit. In these circumstances, hard work is necessary, and is likely to bring a great reward. But what will happen when the point has been reached where everybody could be comfortable without working long hours?")]

#zh(id: "S019")[#text("就目前而言，这可能是好事。一个拥有丰富自然资源的大国等待着发展，而且必须在很少使用信贷的情况下进行发展。在这种情况下，努力工作是必要的，并且可能会带来丰厚的回报。但是，当达到每个人都无需长时间工作就能感到舒适的地步时，会发生什么呢？")]

#html.p(id: "en-S020")[#text("In the West., we have various ways of dealing with this problem. We have no attempt at economic justice, so that a large proportion of the total produce goes to a small minority of the population, many of whom do no work at all. Owing to the absence of any central control over production, we produce hosts of things that are not wanted, We keep a large percentage of the working population idle because we can dispense with their labour by making the others overwork. When all these methods prove inadequate, we have a war: we cause a number of people to manufacture high explosives, and a number of others to explode them, as if we were children who had just discovered fireworks. By a combination of all these devices we manage, though with difficulty, to keep alive the notion that a great deal of severe manual work must be the lot of the average man.")]

#zh(id: "S020")[#text("在西方，我们有多种方法来处理这个问题。我们没有尝试实现经济正义，因此总产出的很大一部分流向了一小部分人，其中许多人根本不工作。由于缺乏对生产的中央控制，我们生产了许多不需要的东西。我们让很大一部分劳动人口闲置，因为我们可以通过让其他人过度劳累来免除他们的劳动。当所有这些方法都证明不够时，我们就会爆发战争：我们让一些人制造烈性炸药，又让一些人引爆它们，就好像我们是刚刚发现烟花的孩子一样。通过将所有这些设备结合起来，我们虽然很困难，但仍然保持着这样一种观念：大量繁重的体力劳动必须是普通人的责任。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S021")[#text("In Russia, owing to more economic justice and central control over production, the problem will have to be differently solved. The rational solution would be, as soon as the necessaries and elementary comforts can be provided for all, to reduce the hours of labour gradually, allowing a popular vote to decide, at each stage, whether more leisure or more goods were to be preferred. But, having taught the supreme virtue of hard work, it is difficult to see how the authorities can aim at a paradise in which there will be much leisure and little work. It seems more likely that they will find continually fresh schemes, by which present leisure is to be sacrificed to future productivity. I read recently of an ingenious plan put forward by Russian engineers, for making the White Sea and the northern coasts of Siberia warm, by putting a dam across the Kara Sea. An admirable project, but liable to postpone proletarian comfort for a generation, while the nobility of toil is being displayed amid the ice-fields and snowstorms of the Arctic Ocean. This sort of thing, if it happens, will be the result of regarding the virtue of hard work as an end in itself, rather than as a means to a state of affairs in which it is no longer needed.")]

#zh(id: "S021")[#text("在俄罗斯，由于更多的经济正义和对生产的中央控制，这个问题必须以不同的方式解决。合理的解决方案是，一旦能够为所有人提供必需品和基本舒适，就逐步减少劳动时间，让民众投票决定在每个阶段是选择更多的休闲还是更多的商品。但是，在教导了勤奋工作的最高美德之后，很难看出当局如何能够建立一个多闲少劳的天堂。似乎更有可能的是，他们会不断找到新的计划，通过这些计划，可以牺牲当前的休闲来换取未来的生产力。我最近读到俄罗斯工程师提出了一项巧妙的计划，通过在喀拉海修建一座水坝来使白海和西伯利亚北部海岸变暖。这是一个令人钦佩的项目，但很可能会推迟一代人的无产阶级舒适生活，而在北冰洋的冰原和暴风雪中却展现出辛劳的高贵。这种事情如果发生的话，将是把努力工作的美德本身视为目的的结果，而不是视为不再需要努力的一种手段。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S022")[#text("The fact is that moving matter about, while a certain amount of it is necessary to our existence, is emphatically not one of the ends of human life. If it were, we should have to consider every navvy superior to Shakespeare. We have been misled in this matter by two causes. One is the necessity of keeping the poor contented, which has led the rich, for thousands of years, to preach the dignity of labour, while taking care themselves to remain undignified in this respect. The other is the new pleasure in mechanism, which makes us delight in the astonishingly clever changes that we can produce on the earth's surface. Neither of these motives makes any great appeal to the actual worker. If you ask him what he thinks the best part of his life, he is not likely to say: \"I enjoy manual work because it makes me feel that I am fulfilling man's noblest task, and because I like to think how much man can transform his planet. It is true that my body demands periods of rest, which I have to fill in as best I may, but I am never so happy as when the morning comes and I can return to the toil from which my contentment springs.\" I have never heard working men say this sort of thing. They consider work, as it should be considered, a necessary means to a livelihood, and it is from their leisure hours that they derive whatever happiness they may enjoy.")]

#zh(id: "S022")[#text("事实是，虽然物质的移动对于我们的存在是必要的，但它显然不是人类生活的目的之一。如果是这样，我们就不得不认为每一个海军都比莎士比亚优越。我们在这件事上被误导有两个原因。一是让穷人感到满足的必要性，这使得富人几千年来一直宣扬劳动的尊严，同时又小心翼翼地在这方面保持不尊严。另一个是机械带来的新乐趣，它让我们为我们能够在地球表面产生的令人惊讶的巧妙变化而感到高兴。这些动机对于实际工作者来说都没有多大吸引力。如果你问他认为自己生命中最美好的部分是什么，他不太可能说：“我喜欢体力劳动，因为这让我觉得我正在完成人类最崇高的任务，而且因为我喜欢思考人类可以在多大程度上改变他的星球。确实，我的身体需要一段时间的休息，我必须尽我所能地填补这些时间，但我从来没有像早晨来临时那样高兴，我可以回到劳作中，而我的满足感正是从劳作中产生的。”我从来没有听工人说过这样的话。他们认为工作，正如应该被认为的那样，是谋生的必要手段，他们从闲暇时间中获得了他们可能享受的任何幸福。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S023")[#text("It will be said that, while a little leisure is pleasant, men would not know how to fill their days if they had only four hours of work out of the twenty-four. In so far as this is true in the modern world, it is a condemnation of our civilization; it would not have been true at any earlier period. There was formerly a capacity for lightheartedness and play which has been to some extent inhibited by the cult of efficiency. The modern man thinks that everything ought to be done for the sake of something else, and never for its own sake. Serious-minded persons, for example, are continually condemning the habit of going to the cinema, and telling us that it leads the young into crime. But all the work that goes to producing a cinema is respectable, because it is work, and because it brings a money profit. The notion that the desirable activities are those that bring a profit has made everything topsy-turvy. The butcher who provides you with meat and the baker who provides you with bread are praiseworthy, because they are making money; but when you enjoy the food they have provided, you are merely frivolous, unless you cat only to get strength for your work. Broadly speaking, it is held that getting money is good and spending money is bad. Seeing that they are two sides of one transaction, this is absurd; one might as well maintain that keys are good, but keyholes are bad. Whatever merit there may be in the production of goods must be entirely derivative from the advantage to be obtained by consuming them. The individual, in our society, works for profit; but the social purpose of his work lies in the consumption of what he produces. It is this divorce between the individual and the social purpose of production that makes it so difficult for men to think clearly in a world in which profit-making is the incentive to industry. We think too much of production, and too little of consumption. One result is that we attach too little importance to enjoyment and simple happiness, and that we do not judge production by the pleasure that it gives to the consumer.")]

#zh(id: "S023")[#text("有人会说，虽然有一点闲暇是令人愉快的，但如果人们在二十四小时中只有四个小时工作，他们就不知道如何打发日子。就现代世界而言，这是对我们文明的谴责。在任何早期时期，情况都不会如此。以前有一种轻松愉快的能力，但这种能力在某种程度上受到了对效率的崇拜的抑制。现代人认为，做任何事情都应该是为了其他事情，而不是为了事情本身。例如，严肃的人不断谴责去看电影的习惯，并告诉我们它会导致年轻人犯罪。但制作电影的所有工作都是值得尊敬的，因为它是工作，而且因为它带来金钱利润。 “理想的活动就是那些能带来利润的活动”这一观念让一切都变得天翻地覆。为你提供肉的屠夫和为你提供面包的面包师是值得称赞的，因为他们在赚钱；但当你享受他们提供的食物时，你就只是轻浮了，除非你只是为了为你的工作获得力量。从广义上讲，人们认为赚钱是好事，花钱是坏事。看到它们是一笔交易的两个方面，这是荒谬的；人们不妨认为钥匙是好的，但锁孔是坏的。无论商品生产有什么优点，都必须完全源自商品生产的优势。通过消耗它们而获得。在我们的社会中，个人是为了利润而工作；但他工作的社会目的在于消费他所生产的东西。正是这种个人与社会生产目的之间的脱节，使得人们很难在一个以盈利为工业动力的世界中清晰地思考。我们对生产考虑太多，而对消费考虑太少。结果之一是我们太不重视享受和简单的幸福，并且我们不根据生产给消费者带来的快乐来判断生产。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S024")[#text("When I suggest that working hours should be reduced to four, I am not meaning to imply that all the remaining time should necessarily be spent in pure frivolity. I mean that four hours' work a day should entitle a man to the necessities and elementary comforts of life, and that the rest of his time should be his to use as he might see fit. It is an essential part of any such social system that education should be carried further than it usually is at present, and should aim, in part, at providing tastes which would enable a man to use leisure intelligently. I am not thinking mainly of the sort of things that would be considered \"highbrow.\" Peasant dances have died out except in remote rural areas, but the impulses which caused them to be cultivated must still exist in human nature. The pleasures of urban populations have become mainly passive: seeing cinemas, watching football matches, listening to the radio, and so on. This results from the fact that their active energies are fully taken up with work; if they had more leisure, they would again enjoy pleasures in which they took an active part.")]

#zh(id: "S024")[#text("当我建议将工作时间减少到四小时时，我的意思并不是说所有剩余的时间都必须用于纯粹的无聊时光。我的意思是，每天工作四个小时应该使一个人能够享受生活的必需品和基本的舒适，而他的其余时间应该由他按照他认为合适的方式使用。任何此类社会制度的一个重要组成部分是，教育应该比目前更进一步，并且应该部分旨在提供使人们能够明智地利用闲暇的品味。我主要考虑的并不是那些被认为是“曲高和寡”的事情。农民舞蹈除了在偏远的农村地区外已经消亡，但促使农民舞蹈产生的冲动一定仍然存在于人性中。城市人口的快乐主要变得被动：看电影、看足球比赛、听广播等等。这是因为他们的积极能量完全投入到工作中；如果他们有更多闲暇，他们就会再次享受他们积极参与的快乐。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S025")[#text("In the past, there was a small leisure class and a larger working class. The leisure class enjoyed advantages for which there was no basis in social justice; this necessarily made it oppressive, limited its sympathies, and caused it to invent theories by which to justify its privileges. These facts greatly diminished its excellence, but in spite of this drawback it contributed nearly the whole of what we call civilization. It cultivated the arts and discovered the sciences; it wrote the books, invented the philosophies, and refined social relations. Even the liberation of the oppressed has usually been inaugurated from above. Without the leisure class, mankind would never have emerged from barbarism.")]

#zh(id: "S025")[#text("过去，有一个较小的休闲阶级和一个较大的工人阶级。有闲阶级享有的优势是没有社会正义基础的。这必然使其具有压迫性，限制其同情心，并导致其发明理论来证明其特权的正当性。这些事实大大削弱了它的卓越性，但尽管有这个缺点，它几乎贡献了我们所说的文明的全部。它培育了艺术并发现了科学；它着书立说，发明哲学，改善社会关系。甚至连被压迫者的解放通常也是自上而下开始的。如果没有有闲阶级，人类永远不可能摆脱野蛮状态。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S026")[#text("The method of a hereditary leisure class without duties was, however, extraordinarily wasteful. None of the members of the class had been taught to be industrious, and the class as a whole was not exceptionally intelligent. The class might produce one Darwin, but against him had to be set tens of thousands of country gentlemen who never thought of anything more intelligent than fox-hunting and punishing poachers. At present, the universities are supposed to provide, in a more systematic way, what the leisure class provided accidentally and as a by-product. This is a great improvement, but it has certain drawbacks. University life is so different from life in the world at large that men who live in an academic milieu tend to be unaware of the preoccupations and problems of ordinary men and women; moreover their ways of expressing themselves are usually such as to rob their opinions of the influence that they ought to have upon the general public. Another disadvantage is that in universities studies are organized, and the man who thinks of some original line of research is likely to be discouraged. Academic institutions, therefore, useful as they are, are not adequate guardians of the interests of civilization in a world where everyone outside their walls is too busy for unutilitarian pursuits.")]

#zh(id: "S026")[#text("然而，没有义务的世袭有闲阶级的方法是极其浪费的。班上没有一个人被教导要勤奋，整个班级也不是特别聪明。这个班级可能会产生一个达尔文，但必须有数以万计的乡村绅士来反对他，他们从来没有想到过比猎狐和惩罚偷猎者更聪明的事情。目前，大学应该以更系统的方式提供有闲阶层偶然提供的副产品。这是一个很大的改进，但也有一定的缺点。大学生活与整个世界的生活如此不同，以至于生活在学术环境中的男性往往不了解普通男性和女性的关注点和问题；此外，他们的表达方式通常会剥夺他们的观点对公众应有的影响力。另一个缺点是，大学里的研究是有组织的，想要进行一些原创研究的人很可能会灰心丧气。因此，尽管学术机构很有用，但在这个世界上，学术机构并不足以保护文明的利益，因为在这个世界上，学术机构之外的每个人都忙于无功利的追求。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S027")[#text("In a world where no one is compelled to work more than four hours a day, every person possessed of scientific curiosity will be able to indulge it, and every painter will be able to paint without starving, however excellent his pictures may be. Young writers will not be obliged to draw attention to themselves by sensational pot-boilers, with a view to acquiring the economic independence needed for monumental works, for which, when the time at last comes, they will have lost the taste and the capacity. Men who, in their professional work, have become interested in some phase of economics or government, will be able to develop their ideas without the academic detachment that makes the work of university economists often seem lacking in reality. Medical men will have time to learn about the progress of medicine, teachers will not be exasperatedly struggling to teach by routine methods things which they learnt in their youth, which may, in the interval,, have been proved to be untrue.")]

#zh(id: "S027")[#text("在一个没有人每天工作超过四个小时的世界里，每个具有科学好奇心的人都可以尽情享受，每个画家无论画得多么出色，都可以不挨饿地作画。年轻作家不必通过耸人听闻的大锅饭来吸引自己的注意力，以期获得不朽作品所需的经济独立，而当这一时刻最终到来时，他们将失去品味和能力。在专业工作中对经济学或政府的某个阶段产生兴趣的人将能够在没有学术超脱的情况下发展自己的想法，而学术超脱使得大学经济学家的工作往往显得缺乏现实。医务人员将有时间了解医学的进步，教师将不会费力地用常规方法教授他们年轻时学到的东西，而这些东西可能在这段时间被证明是不真实的。")]

#html.p(id: "en-S028")[#text("Above all, there will be happiness and joy of life, instead of frayed nerves, weariness, and dyspepsia. The work exacted will be enough to make leisure delightful, but not enough to produce exhaustion. Since men will not be tired in their spare time, they will not demand only such amusements as are passive and vapid. At least i per cent will probably devote the time not spent in professional work to pursuits of some public importance, and, since they will not depend upon these pursuits for their livelihood, their originality will be unhampered, and there will be no need to conform to the standards set by elderly pundits. But it is not only in these exceptional cases that the advantages of leisure will appear. Ordinary men and women, having the opportunity of a happy life, will become more kindly and less persecuting and less inclined to view others with suspicion. The taste for war will die out, partly for this reason, and partly because it will involve long and severe work for all. Good nature is, of all moral qualities, the one that the world needs most, and good nature is the result of ease and security, not of a life of arduous struggle. Modern methods of production have given us the possibility of ease and security for all; we have chosen, instead, to have overwork for some and starvation for the others. Hitherto we have continued to be as energetic as we were before there were machines; in this we have been foolish, but there is no reason to go on being foolish for ever.")]

#zh(id: "S028")[#text("最重要的是，生活将充满幸福和快乐，而不是紧张、疲倦和消化不良。所付出的努力足以使休闲愉快，但不足以产生疲惫。人在闲暇时不会感到疲倦，所以他们不会只要求那些消极、乏味的娱乐。至少有百分之一的人可能会把没有花在专业工作上的时间投入到对某些公共重要性的追求上，而且，由于他们不会依赖这些追求来谋生，他们的原创性将不会受到阻碍，也没有必要遵守年长专家设定的标准。但休闲的优势不仅仅在这些特殊情况下才会显现出来。普通的男人和女人，有了幸福生活的机会，就会变得更加仁慈，更少迫害，更少用怀疑的眼光看待别人。对战争的兴趣将会消失，部分是因为这个原因，部分是因为战争将涉及所有人的长期而艰苦的工作。在所有道德品质中，善良的本性是世界最需要的，而善良的本性是安逸和安全的结果，而不是艰苦奋斗生活的结果。现代生产方法使我们能够为所有人提供轻松和安全的生活；相反，我们选择让一些人过度劳累，另一些人挨饿。迄今为止，我们仍然像机器出现之前一样充满活力。在这方面我们一直很愚蠢，但没有理由继续愚蠢下去。曾经。")]

